    "body": "## Problem\n\nA mixed batch — decoders plus a prefill chunk — can never replay a full CUDA graph. `get_uniform_decode_token_count` returns `None` as soon as `has_prefill` is true, so the handful of decode tokens in that step falls back to eager execution. Measured on TP4 with 1024-token chunks:\n\n| batch | cpu | gpu |\n|---|---|---|\n| pure prefill, 1 req, 1024 tok | 232 ms | 268 ms |\n| mixed, 3 req, 1040 tok | 343 ms | 350 ms |\n\nThat is **+108 ms host and +82 ms GPU for 16 decode tokens**, and the host cost dominates — it is paid on every step the batch is mixed.\n\n## Change\n\nA time-credit allocator decides, per scheduler step, whether it runs as pure prefill or pure decode. It governs scheduling only: a prefill chunk is sized to finish inside one phase, so the decode wait the guard promises (`max_decode_wait_ms` + one prefill step) still holds.\n\n### Design notes\n\n**Only the phase's own elapsed time is fed back.** Dividing a whole mixed step by the prefill tokens alone inflates the apparent per-token cost roughly 10× — a good way to make the controller starve itself.\n\n**The decision compares against an absolute zero, never against a quantity derived from the allocator's own output.** A gate of the form `tokens < budget * k` compares against the *current* budget: once it reaches the floor, the gate always passes and the collapse sustains itself.\n\n**Growth and shrink are symmetric, with an absolute constant floor.** Capping growth while leaving shrink unbounded turns any collapse into a one-way ratchet.\n\n**Sweep steps split the output budget evenly across pending decode requests.** Walking `self.running` in arrival order lets long-output requests at the front consume the whole budget, so a request that just finished prefill sits unscheduled at the back and the client sees its prompt complete with no output.\n\n**A DECODE phase admits no new requests** and skips requests that still owe prefill work — either would turn the step back into a mixed batch and lose the graph.\n\n## Relationship to MixedPrefillBudget\n\nBoth address the same defect from opposite sides, so they are mutually exclusive: `control_mixed_prefill` is forced `False` when phase separation is on. The guardrails above are specifically the failure modes observed with the budget controller.\n\n## Configuration\n\nOn by default. `VLLM_SM70_PHASE_SEPARATION=0` restores mixed batching for A/B. `VLLM_SM70_PHASE_LOG_INTERVAL=N` logs the realised decode:prefill pattern every N steps (the knob is a time share, the log converts it to steps).\n\n## Testing\n\n- 4-way decode under sustained load: latency stable, graph replay confirmed in the profile.\n- Prefill throughput unchanged: the same tokens per unit time, just not interleaved.\n- `VLLM_SM70_PHASE_SEPARATION=0` returns behaviour to identical pre-change numbers.\n- Empty-step spin covered: a step that schedules nothing switches phase next step rather than idling.",
